ef 17592186194603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198662 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198662 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198662 #edb/ref 1071 "A book has a mass of $1\\,\\text{lb}.$ What is this mass in ounces?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198662 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89a7edee-cd9c-55e2-972b-fbfa2cb0a4f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198662 #edb/ref 1074 "A table showing the customary units of mass is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\text{ton}$ | $1\\,\\text{ton}=2,000\\,\\text{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\text{lb}$ | $1\\,\\text{lb}=16\\,\\text{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\text{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, $1\\,\\text{lb}$ is equivalent to $16\\,\\text{oz}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198671 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198671 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198671 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we want to measure the mass of a feather. Which metric unit could we use?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198671 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4656a6b1-fa15-5156-a6f3-5a791bdafd94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198671 #edb/ref 1074 "A table showing the metric units of mass is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol |\n| --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\text{kg}$ |\n| Gram | $\\text{g}$ |\n| Milligram | $\\text{mg}$ |\n\nTherefore, from the given options, the only unit of mass is $\\text{mg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199349 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199349 #edb/ref 1061 45039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199349 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "375871bc-899c-5a27-b8eb-fd1041399f58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199349 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199353 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199353 #edb/ref 1061 45088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c48ca90-f579-5512-92f0-b7c2525cca1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199354 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199354 #edb/ref 1061 45090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199354 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a0b7db5-0023-5d56-b54d-db2933fe9960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199354 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199358 #edb/ref 1061 45096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74eaf0de-fbc6-5a80-ab6d-c4b4cf46dec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199359 #edb/ref 1061 45097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1da9dd54-2e79-527a-b1e6-5c97bb442520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199363 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199363 #edb/ref 1061 45119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199363 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffc96bd1-e431-5266-84b4-0ab22768851f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199363 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199391 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199391 #edb/ref 1061 45704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199391 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09d54f2a-9e5f-5ab1-828e-02e12c87f8aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199391 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199492 #edb/ref 1064 "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. \n\nThe **mass** of an object measures how much \"stuff\" (or **matter**) an object contains. The mass of an object is connected to its weight. We use **mass units** to specify an object's mass. \n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of mass is the **gram,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{g}$.\" The object below has a mass of exactly $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n![](images/dd/dd6ce9605969965944c1b9a7a87e4064bc1c64fb7195ee58b27e744a5e6dfd2b.png)\n\nWe can measure the mass of an object using **weighing scales.** To measure an object's mass, we first place it on one side of the weighing scales. Then, we put $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the other side until the scales balance perfectly. \n\n![](images/dd/dd4da91ec2ee27333843532cbf303da3dbe5eac388c8b14b538b8852159b0f76.png)\n\nIn this example, we have an object on the left scale and sixteen $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the right scale. Since the scales are perfectly balanced, the object's mass is $16\\,\\textrm g.$\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for mass units are shown below:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest (or \"heaviest\") unit $(\\textrm{kg})$ to the smallest (or \"lightest\") unit $(\\textrm{mg})$:\n\n- A regular bag of sugar has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- A single postage stamp has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n- A single grain of sand has a mass of around $50\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol for mass ends in \"$\\textrm{g}$\", the base unit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199492 #edb/ref 1065 7782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199492 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6d1214d5-e0a9-5ffd-8b79-73c1b78305b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199492 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199496 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**. \n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\textrm{ton}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\textrm{lb}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\textrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}.$\n\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}.$\n\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}.$\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199496 #edb/ref 1065 7798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "71c4ce6d-783d-5c7a-ac8e-015cc5bde4c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199496 #edb/ref 1067 "Customary Units of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199514 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{1\\,000\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199514 #edb/ref 1065 7944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199514 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c656aea-de27-5e2c-9a0f-d9a229d4e0c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199514 #edb/ref 1067 "The Prefix System for Metric Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431542 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{ms}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02c2ce8a-393d-5bd5-8113-38b45d83b6e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431543 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{mg}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431543 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431543 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "052f5b12-938b-5e54-a28b-4bf67bc423b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431544 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431544 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431544 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0dcbaaef-fdb8-5857-b7b8-192113235d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431545 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{oz}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431545 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431545 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1